{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"explain-reduced-availability-to-friends","kind":"prompt","title":"Explain reduced availability to friends","description":"Helps you tell friends you will be less available for a while, after a baby, while caring for someone, during burnout, study or a hard time, so they understand and the friendships survive.","category":"interpersonal-communication","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["build"],"role":["individual","parent","student"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text"],"output":["message","plan"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"quick","interaction":"one-shot","model_tier":"mid","reasoning":"optional","level":"beginner","depth":"specific","tags":["friendships","life-changes","staying-in-touch","burnout","expectations"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["plan-keep-in-touch-rhythm","write-message-to-cancel-plans","set-boundary"],"personas":["communication-coach"]},"args":[{"name":"change","description":"What is changing and for roughly how long, how much you want to share, and what you can still manage (a text now and then, a short walk, nothing for a while).","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"friends","description":"Who you are telling, for example \"my close group chat of five\", \"one best friend who will take it personally\", \"wider friends and acquaintances\".","type":"text","required":true}],"output_contract":{"format":"markdown","sections":["What to share","Messages","What you can still offer","If they react badly"]},"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"<context>\nFriendships rarely end in an argument; they fade when one person goes quiet and the other reads it as rejection. Saying something early, even briefly, changes how silence is read. A good message tells friends three things: that something is going on (as much or as little detail as you choose), that it is not about them, and what contact is still possible, ideally something small and concrete (\"I'm rubbish at replying right now, but voice notes are great\" or \"I can't do evenings out, but a Sunday walk once a month would be lovely\"). Over-promising (\"I'll be back to normal soon!\") sets up disappointment. Close friends usually deserve a personal message rather than a group announcement. People also help more when told what helps: dropping off food, not expecting replies, inviting you anyway.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nHelp me tell my friends I will be less available.\n\n<change>\n{{change}}\n</change>\n\n<friends>\n{{friends}}\n</friends>\n\n1. If what is changing or how long it might last is unclear, make a sensible assumption, say it, and continue.\n2. Help me decide what to share with each group of friends: enough for them to understand, nothing I am not ready to share. Give a one-line version for people who do not need detail.\n3. Write the messages: a personal one for the closest friend or friends, and a shorter one for a group chat or wider circle if relevant. Each says it is not about them, says what I can still do, and invites them to keep inviting me. Under about 80 words each.\n4. List small, realistic ways I can stay connected for my situation, and how friends can help if they ask.\n5. Give replies for when a friend takes it personally, pushes, or goes quiet, and a short message to send later when I have more capacity again.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- No over-apologising or over-explaining; keep it warm and simple.\n- Do not promise a return date I did not give.\n- Respect privacy: if the reason is health, a relationship ending, or mental health, offer wording that shares only what I choose.\n- If the person mentions thoughts of suicide or self-harm, harming someone else, abuse, or being in danger, stop the exercise. Respond with care, tell them they deserve support now, and point them to local emergency services or a crisis line in their country. If you do not know their country, ask, and mention that local emergency numbers work everywhere.\n- You are a supportive tool, not therapy. For ongoing distress, low mood that lasts, or anything that disrupts daily life, encourage them to talk to a doctor or a licensed mental-health professional.\n- Never shame, diagnose, or tell someone what they \"really\" feel. Reflect back what they said and offer, rather than impose, next steps.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## What to share\nWho gets how much detail.\n## Messages\nThe personal message, then the group or wider one.\n## What you can still offer\nShort list, plus how friends can help.\n## If they react badly\nTable: If they say… | You can say… Then a \"back again\" message for later.\n</output_format>","steps":[]}
